points.explanation:
The causative means someone **makes** (or lets) another person do something. The causer is the subject.

How to make it:

- {る}-verbs: drop {る}, add {させる}: {食[た]べさせる}, {見[み]させる}
- {う}-verbs: last sound to the a-row + {せる}: {書[か]かせる}, {待[ま]たせる}, {飲[の]ませる}, {帰[かえ]らせる}; {言[い]う} → {言[い]わせる}
- {する} → {させる}, {来[く]る} → {来[こ]させる}

Particles for the person who is made to act:

- If the verb already has an object with {を}, the person takes {に}: {子[こ]どもに野菜[やさい]を食[た]べさせる} 'make the child eat vegetables.'
- With intransitive verbs, the person usually takes {を}: {部下[ぶか]を出張[しゅっちょう]させる} 'send a subordinate on a business trip.'

It also covers causing emotions: {母[はは]を心配[しんぱい]させる} 'make my mother worry'. And the everyday apology {お待[ま]たせしました} / {待[ま]たせてごめん} 'Sorry to keep you waiting' uses it too.

Combine causative and passive and you get 'be made to do': {私[わたし]は母[はは]に野菜[やさい]を食[た]べさせられた} 'I was made to eat vegetables by my mother.' The subject is the person who had to do it, and the one who forced them is marked with {に}. It always carries a feeling of reluctance.

How to make it: take the causative ({させる}) and make it passive ({させられる}).

- {る}-verbs: {食[た]べさせられる}, {覚[おぼ]えさせられる}
- {う}-verbs: the full form is {書[か]かせられる}, but the short form {書[か]かされる} is far more common: {飲[の]まされる}, {待[ま]たされる}, {行[い]かされる}. Exception: verbs ending in {す} only use the long form: {話[はな]させられる}.
- {する} → {させられる}, {来[く]る} → {来[こ]させられる}

You'll hear this a lot when people complain about school, parents and work.
